  "justification": "**Operational Excellence**: The proposed architecture implements automated scaling policies tied to CloudWatch metrics (CPU utilization and request count), enabling the system to respond to demand without manual intervention. ElastiCache reduces database load by caching frequent queries and session data, improving response times and reducing operational toil. Point-in-time recovery with 7-day retention enables restoration to any point within the RPO window, supporting both disaster recovery and operational error correction.\n\n**Security**: The architecture maintains defense-in-depth with private subnets for compute and database tiers, public subnets only for the ALB, and CloudFront providing DDoS protection and WAF integration capability. The NAT Gateway enables secure outbound connections for updates and external API calls without exposing instances. ElastiCache in-transit encryption and RDS encryption at rest protect sensitive data. IAM roles for EC2 instances follow least-privilege principles for S3 and RDS access.\n\n**Reliability**: Multi-AZ RDS with automated failover meets requirement N2 for database high availability, achieving RTO under 1 hour (typically 1-2 minutes for automatic failover). Point-in-time recovery with 7-day retention and automated backups every 5 minutes satisfies the 5-minute RPO requirement (N3). The minimum of 2 t3.medium instances across 2 AZs ensures the web tier survives a single AZ failure while maintaining 320 req/s capacity (2 × 160), sufficient for normal load with headroom. During peak events (750 req/s required), Auto Scaling provisions up to 6 instances providing 960 req/s capacity with 28% overhead for fault tolerance. The two-node ElastiCache cluster provides cache redundancy.\n\n**Performance Efficiency**: Upgrading from t3.micro (40 req/s) to t3.medium (160 req/s) right-sizes compute for the workload, reducing the instance count needed at peak from 19 instances to 5 instances, improving cost efficiency and reducing management overhead. ElastiCache dramatically reduces database query latency for repeated reads (sessions, popular content metadata), improving user experience and reducing RDS load by 60-80% for read-heavy photo-sharing workloads. CloudFront 50% cache hit rate eliminates half the origin requests entirely. The architecture scales horizontally for stateless web tier and uses managed services (RDS, ElastiCache) that scale vertically when needed.\n\n**Cost Optimization**: At $629.66 monthly, the proposal stays within the $750 budget (N5) while delivering 10× request capacity. ElastiCache ($99.28) is cost-effective insurance: by reducing database load, it defers expensive database scaling and prevents performance degradation under load. The t3.medium instances use burstable performance to handle variable load cost-effectively. The maximum ASG size of 6 instances caps peak-hour costs while providing adequate capacity (960 req/s vs 750 required). CloudFront reduces origin load and data transfer costs. S3 lifecycle policies could further reduce storage costs as photo archives age.\n\n**Sustainability**: Right-sizing to t3.medium reduces the total instance-hours needed monthly from potential overprovisioning. Auto Scaling ensures instances run only when needed, reducing idle capacity during off-peak (725 hours monthly). ElastiCache reduces redundant database queries, lowering overall computational waste. Multi-AZ deployment, while using more resources, prevents the carbon cost of emergency recovery procedures and replacement infrastructure provisioning.",
  "comparison": "The original architecture at $175.13/month handles 2,920 requests/month (2 × t3.micro × 730h) with 80 req/s sustained capacity, adequate for the previous ~5 req/s average load but unable to scale to the new 750 req/s peak requirement without provisioning 19 t3.micro instances. It lacks caching, forcing all non-CloudFront-cached requests to hit the database. The proposed architecture at $629.66/month introduces ElastiCache for performance and database protection, upgrades to t3.medium instances for better performance density (4× capacity per instance), and scales to 6 instances maximum to handle peak load with fault tolerance. The original would violate the peak capacity requirement (N1) and would cost more if scaled to meet it (19 × t3.micro × 730h × $0.0104 = $144.25 for compute alone during scaling events, plus increased ALB LCU costs). The proposed architecture meets all requirements N1-N6, improves reliability through caching redundancy, and provides sustainable scaling headroom within budget."
