ccidental deletion. CloudFront global edge network absorbs 50% of traffic, reducing origin load and improving global availability.\n\nPERFORMANCE EFFICIENCY: t3.medium instances provide 160 RPS capacity per instance at 70% CPU, efficiently handling steady-state load with 2 instances while scaling to 6 instances for promotional peaks. ElastiCache t3.medium nodes cache session data and frequently accessed metadata, reducing database round-trips from milliseconds to microseconds and offloading read-intensive queries. CloudFront edge caching reduces origin requests by 50% and delivers content from locations nearest to users, reducing latency from hundreds of milliseconds to tens of milliseconds for global audiences. The ALB distributes traffic evenly across healthy instances, preventing hotspots. Auto Scaling responds to demand within minutes, adding capacity before performance degrades.\n\nCOST OPTIMIZATION: The architecture targets the $750 monthly budget ceiling at $749, delivering 3.7x cost increase for 8x traffic growth (5M to 40M monthly requests) and 150x peak capacity increase (5 RPS to 750 RPS). t3.medium instances provide better price-performance than t3.small for the 160 RPS per-instance requirement, avoiding the need for more numerous smaller instances. ElastiCache investment ($99.28/month) reduces RDS IOPS costs and enables smaller database instances by offloading read queries. Minimum ASG configuration of 2 instances eliminates waste during off-peak hours while maintaining high availability. CloudFront caching reduces origin compute costs by 50%. Single NAT gateway balances cost against availability risk for non-critical outbound traffic. Automated scaling eliminates overprovisioning—capacity expands only during promotional events, then contracts to minimum.",
